 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the highway law, in relation to designating a portion of the\nstate highway system as the \"Medal of Honor Recipient Francis S. Currey\nMemorial Highway\"",
    "basePrintNo": "S7297",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7297-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §344-m, Hway L",
    "lawSection": "Highway Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7297\n\nSPONSOR: BRESLIN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the highway law, in relation to designating a portion of\nthe state highway system as the \"Medal of Honor Recipient Francis S.\nCurrey Memorial Highway\"\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nAdds new section 344-m to the Highway Law designating state Route 32\nbetween its intersection with Bender Lanbe, and its intersection with\nElsmere Avenue in the Town of Bethlehem, County of Albany as the \"Medal\nof Honor Recipient Francis S. Currey Memorial Highway\"\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis bill is being introduced at the request of the Commander of the\nAmerican Legion Post 1040 in Delmar, and at the request of Francis S.\nCurrey's Family.\n\nOne week after graduating High School in 1943, Frank enlisted in the\nU.S. Army. Sergeant Currey and his platoon were assigned to defend the\nsmall Belgian town of Malmedy with little more than small arms.  Facing\na number of German tanks intent on flanking his battalion, Currey\nrepeatedly braved enemy fire to inflict heavy losses on the enemy,\nrescued five American soldiers that had been pinned down and repelled\nthe enemy advance. For his valor he was awarded the U.S.  Congressional\nMedal of Honor. In addition, he was presented with the Belgian Order of\nLeopold, a Silver Star, Bronze Star and three Purple Hearts. He was also\na Charter Member of the Congressional Medal of Honor Society. After the\nwar Frank worked as a counselor at the Stratton VA Medical Center in\nAlbany for 30 years retiring in 1980.  Sadly, Francis Currey passed away\nin October 2019 in Selkirk, NY, but it is intended that is legacy live\non forever.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
